{"name":"Bunny Ears","hex":"#f9d9d2","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bunny ears","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hand gesture in which the index and middle fingers are raised and parted, while the other fingers are clenched, used behind the head of a subject of a photograph.","Synonym of rabbit ears (“a double television aerial”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bunny_ears"}],"entries":[{"title":"Opuntia microdasys","blocks":[{"text":"Opuntia microdasys is a species of flowering plant in the cactus family, Cactaceae. It is native and endemic to central and northern Mexico."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Opuntia_microdasys_3.jpg/330px-Opuntia_microdasys_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opuntia_microdasys","wikidata":"Q310523"},{"title":"Rabbit","blocks":[{"text":"Rabbits, or bunnies, are small mammals in the family Leporidae, which is in the order Lagomorpha. They are familiar throughout the world as a small herbivore, a prey animal, a domesticated form of livestock, and a pet, having a widespread effect on ecologies and cultures. The most widespread rabbit genera are Oryctolagus and Sylvilagus. The former, Oryctolagus, includes the European rabbit, Oryctolagus cuniculus, which is the ancestor of the hundreds of breeds of domestic rabbit and has been introduced on every continent except Antarctica. The latter, Sylvilagus, includes over 13 wild rabbit species, among them the cottontails and tapetis. Wild rabbits not included in Oryctolagus and Sylvilagus include several species of limited distribution, including the pygmy rabbit, volcano rabbit, and Sumatran striped rabbit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Oryctolagus_cuniculus_Rcdo.jpg/330px-Oryctolagus_cuniculus_Rcdo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabbit","wikidata":"Q9394"},{"title":"Ear","blocks":[{"text":"In vertebrates, an ear is the organ that enables hearing and body balance using the vestibular system. In humans, the ear is described as having three parts: the outer ear, the middle ear and the inner ear. The outer ear consists of the auricle, the visible outer part, and the ear canal. The middle ear includes the tympanic cavity and the three ossicles. The inner ear sits in the bony labyrinth, and contains structures which are key to several senses: the semicircular canals, which enable balance and eye tracking when moving; the utricle and saccule, which enable balance when stationary; and the cochlea, which enables hearing. The ear canal is cleaned via earwax, which naturally migrates to the auricle."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Human_right_ear_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Human_right_ear_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ear","wikidata":"Q7362"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/OpuntiaMicrodasys1.jpg/500px-OpuntiaMicrodasys1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/OpuntiaMicrodasys1.jpg/1280px-OpuntiaMicrodasys1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/OpuntiaMicrodasys1.jpg/500px-OpuntiaMicrodasys1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Opuntia microdasys specimen naturalized in Kythira. ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOpuntiaMicrodasys1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/OpuntiaMicrodasys.jpg/500px-OpuntiaMicrodasys.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipe